### 2. P1 scale, concurrency, and idempotency

- ~~**Add bounded P1 concurrency with LLM batching.**~~ ✅ Done —
	`ThreadPoolExecutor` for stages 2–3 (`FSR_V2_P1_WORKERS`, default 4).
	LLM batching implemented: `batch_extract_llm_metadata()` sends N docs per
	LLM request (`FSR_V2_P1_LLM_BATCH_SIZE`, default 10; inter-batch delay
	`FSR_V2_P1_LLM_DELAY_S`, default 1s). Batch failures mark all docs in the
	batch as failed; missing rows within a batch are caught individually.
- ~~**Fix: Stage 4 (LLM + per-doc write) blocked behind a full-corpus Stage 2–3
	barrier.**~~ ✅ Done — found during dev backfill validation
	(2026-09-02): `nb_sdg_fsr_v2_metadata.py` submitted the *entire* discovered
	doc set to the `ThreadPoolExecutor` and didn't start Stage 4 until
	`as_completed()` drained for every submitted doc. At tens of thousands of
	docs and 4 workers that barrier alone was many hours with zero visible
	progress, even though Stage 4's per-doc MERGE write was already correct.
	Fixed by interleaving: Stage 4 now fires as soon as `LLM_BATCH_SIZE` docs
	clear Stage 2–3 (via a buffer drained inside the `as_completed()` loop),
	with a final flush for any partial batch after the pool exits. Same total
	throughput, but progress and writes are now continuous, and a mid-run crash
	only loses one in-flight batch instead of the whole run. Verified in the
	sandbox against `ms_test_*` tables (53-doc set, track 4): completions
	landed continuously (7 at ~3min, 27 at ~6min, 53/53 by ~11min) instead of
	one final flush.
