- ~~**Date-filtered docs were re-parsed on every run and the queue never
	drained.**~~ ✅ Done (2026-09-03). Two linked defects found when the first
	full-corpus QA run was started:

	1. **Filtering happened after the full parse.** Discovery has no date filter
	   (it only sees filenames — the year is inside the PDF), so all 50,175 docs
	   were stub-registered and *fully* parsed — every page extracted, boilerplate
	   suppressed, parsed artifact written to the volume — before the year was
	   checked and the doc discarded. Roughly half the corpus is out of range, so
	   about half the parse cost was pure waste.
	2. **Filtered docs stayed `pending` forever.** They were only counted, never
	   given a terminal status. The retry query claims `metadata_status='pending'`,
	   so every later run re-claimed and re-parsed all ~28K of them, and the queue
	   could never reach zero — there was no "backfill complete" signal.

	**Fix:** a cheap `extract_page1_text()` probe reads only page 1 and bails
	before the full parse; out-of-range docs are then written back as
	`metadata_status='date_filtered'` (terminal). The retry query only claims
	`pending`/retry-eligible `failed`, and discovery excludes every id already in
	the table, so a filtered doc is never opened again on later runs.

	Verified on a mixed pre/post-2016 corpus (isolated `ms_test_*`, clean DDL):
	run 1 claimed 54 → 50 completed + 4 `date_filtered` in 681s; **run 2 claimed
	0 docs and finished in 12.9s**, with zero rows left `pending`.

	**P2 is unaffected** — it claims `metadata_status='completed'` only, so
	`date_filtered` is invisible to it.
- ~~**Record the resolved document date, not just the filter outcome.**~~
	✅ Done (2026-09-03) — three columns added to `fsr_metadata_v2`:
	`doc_date` (canonical date, first non-empty of
	`outage_start_date > job_start_date > approved_date > report_issued_date`),
	`doc_year` (the year the filter acted on), and `doc_date_source` (which field
	won, or `missing`; suffixed `:page1_scan` when it came from the pre-parse
	probe). Populated for **both** `completed` and `date_filtered` rows —
	previously a filtered doc had *no* date columns populated at all, so there was
	no record of why it was skipped.

	Makes the filter auditable, gives retrieval/analytics one canonical date
	instead of coalescing four columns, and enables **surgical reclaim**: widening
	the bound only needs to flip the rows that now qualify
	(`... AND doc_year >= <new_min>`) rather than flipping everything and
	re-probing docs that will just be rejected again.
- **Widening the year range later is cheap — but turning the filter off is not
	enough on its own.** Because discovery excludes every id already in the table
	*and* the retry query ignores non-`pending`/`failed` rows, simply clearing
	`FSR_V2_MIN_DOC_YEAR` would leave previously-filtered docs silently skipped
	forever. The reclaim step is a metadata-only UPDATE (no file I/O, no deletes,
	completed docs untouched) — see the backfill runbook.
